There are two aspects to antenna performance: the best gain it can muster over all directions, and the distribution of gain (spherical coverage). In this contribution, we focus on peak gain (10log(N)+Gelememt) because it directly relates to peak EIRP and REFSENS. Note that this quantity also absorbs UE packaging loss, phase shifter non-idealites, etc. Figure 2.1-1: Array gain trend with frequency At 48G, antenna design can get challenging due to possibility of exciting slab modes that can siphon power away from the intended beam if care is not exercised in material choice and dimensions. The figure assumes ‘smooth variation’ over frequency, i.e there are no new mechanisms, like the slab mode, to reduce beam gain further. Extrapolation suggests that array gain can be between 4 and 6 dB lower at 48G than in n258, assuming similar element count. One can also surmise that element gain may be less than 0dB at 48G once packaging and other losses are accounted for.
